once again it's crunch time on Capitol  Hill as lawmakers debate the fate of  daca and look for a way to keep the  government open past a Friday midnight  deadline Senator Marco Rubio joins us  now from Capitol Hill to discuss those  and a lot of other issues thanks for  joining us senator Rubio let's get right  to it the topic of the day is this  immigration impasse once again and for  our viewers out there are you more  attuned to the the current Durbin Graham  version of the immigration bill or would  it be closer to the Republicans who are  called the more hardline people like Tom  cotton or senator Perdue well I'm not I  don't know the details of the Graham  Durban proposal other than what's been  expressed in the media I haven't seen  those details I can tell you what I  believe in I believe we do have to build  a wall and improve our overall security  like at airports with entry/exit I  believe we should end the diversity  lottery I've been saying that for about  five years I believe that we should move  toward a merit-based system of  immigration I believe that we should  figure out a way like the president has  to deal with people that find themselves  or many of the people to find themselves  now under a daca designation and I think  the big debate has been how do you put  all that together and the way I view it  is the president's number-one priority  is border security the Democrats are  insisting on daca and so in order to get  it passed under this sort of framework  that were in now each side is going to  have to get something but what else gets  in there is what the debate has been  about and so I support ending the  diversity lottery I along I think we  should admit people to the United States  based on what they're gonna contribute  to the United States and that's why I've  long called for a merit-based system of  immigration irrespective of what country  here coming from it shouldn't be country  based it should be person based  absolutely and Senator Beck in 2015 you  gave a speech or a it's a back and forth  with Hannity at CPAC and you said I've  learned my lesson on immigration and  your point was that we have to do  enforcement first before we can get to  the other parts you said  and that was obviously because of the  gang of eight that went down in flames  but the gang of eight now it's a gang of  six and you have Lindsey Graham out  there who today said we need different  partners willing partners in the White  House well what of that I mean the White  House is the White House the president  ran on the wall and enforcement and he's  willing to do daca too well just about  anything we pass here you're gonna have  to have him sign it so that's the only  partner at the end of the day that's  going to matter is what the president  will sign and by the way the lesson I  referred to in 2015 was more than just  border security first it was with a  step-by-step approach versus a  comprehensive one on what you try to do  all these things together it says you're  seeing now you're only talking about  five things and they can't put it  together  so that's why I've long believed that  you start with security first that's  something everybody should agree on I  don't know why anybody would argue that  we shouldn't secure our border and  enforce our immigration laws and then  you moved into modernization which are  things like making it merit-based or  getting rid of the diversity lottery  basically going to a person based  immigration system as opposed to a  country or family based one aren't they  doing they are doing amnesty at the same  time not not everybody but they are  doing 800 thousand at the same time so  they're kind of doing and a smaller  version of what you guys tried to do in  2013 and that obviously didn't work out  well and the president's still willing  to give that's the point I mean  everybody involved in this negotiation  is saying we understand that we're gonna  deal with the daca situation but you've  got to do strong enforcement that's  going to happen and one of the  differences between now and 2013 is you  have someone at the White House that  people trust is actually going to do it  that's a big dynamic shift from where we  stood at this time in 2013 arguing about  whether or not the enforcement would  even happen even if we passed the law  people would argue that it wouldn't  happen so that's a difference are you  for it that's an important set of goals  and all of that to come in so I think  keeping the nuclear family is one thing  I think allowing people to claim their  parents or other relatives is not  something that I'd support and and I to  me that's part of moving away from the  family based system and towards the  merit-based system let's talk about what  Jeff Flake is saying tomorrow another  one of your colleagues got a lot of  charges first strip first Trump's a  racist which I assume you don't agree  with that's what Durbin is saying you  don't agree with that right  I've never seen him say or do anything  in front of me that leads me to believe  that you know people have a right to  have whatever opinion they want but but  I certainly have never viewed him that  way yeah I mean I don't know how you sit  down in a room with someone one day and  two days later said well he's a racist  but but now Jeff Flake is giving it  speech tomorrow where he's comparing  Trump's treatment of the press to Joseph  Stalin's  and of course Stalin the Great Purge 20  million to 60 million deaths attributed  to him how do we get here senator well I  think look I don't agree with everything  the president does I bet you you don't  agree with everything the president does  but one of the things that is happening  is exaggeration just like the President  does things from time to time that I  don't agree with or would have done  differently I think the reaction to it  is often very exaggerated I hadn't given  the speech yet so we'll see what he says  tomorrow but I don't think there's any  comparison not just between this  president but any American public figure  with Joseph Stalin but again I don't  know if that's what he's gonna say in a  speech I mean he hasn't given it yet  yeah well he's teased it well let's talk  about what you want to do to prevent  foreign governments from interfering in  American elections you're teaming up  with Chris Van Hollen a Democrat and  what is your goal and what do you intend  to do so there's clearly a Russia  element to it because there's no doubt  the Russians tried to interfere in our  election by the way in my view not  simply to favor a candidate I may have  been what they felt like at the end but  for much of it it was just to sow  discord but my bigger problem we can't  do about that we can only deal with  what's happened in the past  prospectively or you know looking about  retro actively but prophetically the  biggest threat to America's electoral  system and quite frankly America at  large is not Russia it's China  it's China's has basically infiltrated  its way into all of our technologies  across the supply chain they make  anything the Russians can do look like  child's play and that includes stealing  secrets that includes influencing  individuals in American government  through a lot of different methods and  that includes potentially interfering in  an election so I think the biggest  threat moving forward is not the  Russians it's actually the Chinese they  are much larger with a lot more  capability and a lot more money and  that's one of the things I thought about  when  involved in this legislation and any  thoughts about this New York Times piece  that just came out tonight that the  administration is going to be reviewing  possible plans to expand our military  response with nuclear weapons to  potential cyberattacks on water  communications other essential services  that would basically shut down the power  grid etcetera yeah so I promise you that  there's gonna be overreaction to that as  well some of the same things I told you  exaggeration you'll see headlines that  say Trump wants a nuclear attack because  of hacking that that's not what this is  about by the way this doctrine is  actually not new it's just been expanded  and now of course when made public the  bigger thing about this is you talk  about some of these attacks we're  talking about things that would knock  planes out of the sky kill patients and  hospitals wipe out millions of dollars  from bank accounts paralyze our electric  grid I mean I'm talking about massive  loss of life if if the kinds of attacks  that are being tried to anticipate it  here are there are the attacks that  happen people need to know ahead of time  if you do this we're not going to have a  series of town hall meetings to talk  about why did this happen this is what  the response is going to be country's  know what the response is going to be if  they launch a nuclear attack against us  they need to know what that response is  going to be if they launch a massive  life-threatening cyber or irregular  attack as I think a lot of countries  that may not have a nuclear capability  or may have a small nuclear capability  have the ability to expand into this  territory we don't do a better job of  protecting ourselves  senator Rubio thanks so much for  spending some time with us tonight hope  to see you soon Thanks
 

